From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jeremy Grata <jgrata@metropolis.io>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 00:00:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] iommu/arm-smmu-qcom: skip fault-path ATOS when the TBU has no
 power handle

On QCS9075 (SA8775P) native RAW/RDI capture from an IMX678 reliably-
intermittently panics the kernel: the SMMU context-fault handler takes a
synchronous external abort (ESR 0x96000010) inside qsmmuv500_iova_to_phys()
while running the hardware ATOS, and qcom_wdt resets the SoC.

  pc : qsmmuv500_iova_to_phys+0x950/0x1568
  lr : qcom_smmu_context_fault+0x44c/0x890
        arm_smmu_context_fault+0x70/0x548

Root cause: the ATOS pokes a per-TBU DEBUG register block (tbu->base, e.g.
cam_tbu@0x151f1000). On SA8775P *every* qcom,qsmmuv500-tbu DT node - including
cam_tbu - declares no clocks/power-domains/interconnects, so tbu->clk and
tbu->path are NULL and the existing clk_prepare_enable(tbu->clk) /
icc_set_bw(tbu->path) guards are no-ops. The block is instead powered by the
camera subsystem (CAMNOC/camcc). During IFE/RDI teardown that subsystem powers
down; the threaded context-fault IRQ then races the teardown and the ATOS
register access aborts, and the SMMU driver has no handle with which to
re-power the TBU. arm_smmu_rpm_get() covers the apps_smmu, not cam_tbu.

The ATOS result is used only for a diagnostic dev_err() in
qcom_smmu_context_fault(); the fault report (report_iommu_fault + the software
page-table walk) and the FSR clear/RESUME do not depend on it. So when the
driver cannot guarantee the TBU is powered, skip the hardware ATOS rather than
risk a fatal abort. This turns a reboot into a recoverable, reported fault.

The guard is SoC-agnostic: TBUs that do expose a clock or interconnect path
keep the ATOS unchanged.

Signed-off-by: Jeremy Grata <jgrata@metropolis.io>
---
 drivers/iommu/arm/arm-smmu/arm-smmu-qcom-tbu.c | 13 +++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/iommu/arm/arm-smmu/arm-smmu-qcom-tbu.c b/drivers/iommu/arm/arm-smmu/arm-smmu-qcom-tbu.c
--- a/drivers/iommu/arm/arm-smmu/arm-smmu-qcom-tbu.c
+++ b/drivers/iommu/arm/arm-smmu/arm-smmu-qcom-tbu.c
@@ -239,7 +239,20 @@ static phys_addr_t qsmmuv500_iova_to_phys(struct arm_smmu_domain *smmu_domain,
 	tbu = qsmmuv500_find_tbu(qsmmu, sid);
 	if (!tbu)
 		return 0;

+	/*
+	 * If the TBU exposes no clock or interconnect the driver can enable, its
+	 * debug register block is powered by an external subsystem (e.g. the
+	 * camera CAMNOC on SA8775P/QCS9075, where cam_tbu declares no clocks or
+	 * power-domains). That subsystem may be off during IFE/RDI teardown when
+	 * this fault-path ATOS runs, so the tbu->base access below would take a
+	 * synchronous external abort with no way for us to re-power the TBU.
+	 * Skip the hardware ATOS; qcom_smmu_context_fault() still reports the
+	 * fault from the software page-table walk and handles a phys == 0 return.
+	 */
+	if (!tbu->clk && !tbu->path)
+		return 0;
+
 	ret = icc_set_bw(tbu->path, 0, UINT_MAX);
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
--
2.34.1
